# Castafiore - Session Log

## 2026-10-25 — Offline unification (no auto-cache) + downloaded collection sync

### One download index; auto-cache removed
- Two stores wrote into `cache/songs/`: the stream cache (`global.listCacheSong`,
  `isSongCached`, `initCacheSong`, `downloadNextSong`) and the download manager
  (`state.index`). They disagreed on identity (the removal paths used
  `${id}.${format}` while `songCacheKey` appends `-<bitrate>`), and every screen
  re-implemented "offline" (Home/Tracks/Playlists/Album/Playlist/Favorited).
- Downloads are now the only local songs. Deleted `isSongCaching`/`cacheNextSong`/
  `showCache` (settings + globals), `downloadNextSong` from all four player backends
  and `servicePlayback`, `isSongCached`/`getSongCachedInfo`/`deleteSongCache`/
  `getListCacheSong`/`initCacheSong` from `cache.*` (API cache only now).
- New `songFiles.js` (key/path/parse), `coverId.js`, `collectionDiff.js`,
  `useLocalLibrary.js`. `downloadManager` gained `isSongLocal`, `getLocalSongUri`,
  `getLocalSongs`, `getLocalCollections`, `getLocalCollectionSongs`, `getLocalStats`,
  a derived `songIdsBySource` map, and `file` on index entries (a format change no
  longer orphans a download).
- One-time purge: consolidates old per-server folders, deletes files not referenced
  by the index (keeps paused `.part`), guarded by a successful state load + the
  `localFilesMigration` flag. **User decision: delete old auto-cached files**, not
  adopt them.
- Offline selectors are O(N+C) (was O(collections × index) per progress tick);
  `useCachedAndApi`/`getApiCacheFirst`/`getApiNetworkFirst` read cache-only when
  offline (no more timeout hangs on a dead LAN server).
- Web now hides download/offline UI (DownloadButton, Downloads row, offline swap);
  the no-op stub no longer lies. Dead `clearCache`/`clearAllCaches` removed from
  `serviceWorkerRegistration.web.js`.
- Trade-off: `playerLocal.switchServer` can only skip the reload for downloaded
  tracks now (auto-cached streamed tracks are gone).
- Commit `f1dbc8d`; eslint clean; release APK built + installed on R5CY71GYKWF,
  launch verified (album screen, download icon, favourite circle).

### Downloaded collections stay in sync + favourite metadata
- `collectionDiff(expected, collection)` + `downloadManager.syncCollection`:
  adds missing songs, cancels queued items owned by the collection, detaches removed
  ones (file deleted only when no source remains), rewrites `songIds` order/metadata.
- `getCollectionState(source, expectedIds)` exposes `changed` (never on an empty
  expected list, which is indistinguishable from "still loading") and uses
  `songIdsBySource` instead of an index scan.
- `DownloadButton` shows `cloud-upload` when the fresh list differs; tapping syncs.
  `fresh` (from `useCachedAndApi`'s new `mode` arg) must be true, so a cache-only or
  offline list can never trigger a destructive sync. `OptionsFavorited` re-fetches
  `getStarred2` before syncing for the same reason.
- `buildMeta` stores `starred`/`userRating`; `entryToSong` projects them;
  `syncLocalStarred` refreshes downloads from the `getStarred2` cache (wired in
  `useStarredIds`). `PlaylistButton` is now read-only offline instead of hidden.
- `OptionsFavorited` gained "Sync download" for the Favorited collection.
- Offline playlist order follows `collection.songIds` (filtered by source), falling
  back to index order for old records.
- Node harness: diff (add/remove/unchanged/reorder/empty) + offline order/fallback;
  eslint clean; release APK built + installed on R5CY71GYKWF, launch verified.
- **Manual verification owed** (the phone is connected but the active server
  `casa` was unreachable from its network): edit a downloaded playlist, reopen while
  online -> sync icon -> only the delta downloads; star a downloaded song -> offline
  circle updates; Favorited sync entry appears when the starred set changed.

## 2026-09-28 — Multi-select playlist sheet, read-only playlists, player layout
Three user-reported items, plus one that turned out to be a server policy.

### "Add to playlist" is now a membership editor
- Per-playlist circle (empty / filled+check). Playlists already containing the song(s) start
  filled, and **deselecting removes them** — the user's own redefinition, and a genuinely more
  useful model than the old fire-and-forget list. A partially-containing playlist shows an
  empty circle (user decision) and is left alone unless tapped.
- Done button pinned bottom, always enabled (nothing changed -> just closes, no API traffic).
  Applies all selections in one pass, closes the sheet, returns to the originating page.
  Removals confirmed; partial failure reported as a count; no rollback (user decision).
- All five entry points rewired to the one component.
- **The trap:** Subsonic `updatePlaylist` removes by 0-based INDEX, so removals must go
  highest-first — ascending deletes the wrong songs, silently. `updatePlaylistEntries`
  re-fetches for fresh indices, adds first, removes descending. Proved with a simulation.
- Then the sheet was reverted to the plain song-count subtitle on request (the "Auto-imported
  from ..." line was noise); the lock/dim behaviour stayed.

### Why playlist writes were failing: server-side, not our bug
- Symptom: `updatePlaylist` -> `{"code":50,"message":"User is not authorized..."}`.
- Proved it was not our request: a nonexistent playlist id returns **code 70** on Navidrome, so
  existence is checked before authorization and 50 is a deliberate permission decision.
- Root cause: Navidrome flags playlists synced from a `.m3u`/`.nsp` with **`readonly: true`** in
  `getPlaylists` and refuses writes **regardless of owner** — the file on disk is the source of
  truth. The user pushed back with "even if im the correct user", which was right: ownership
  was not the gate. My first explanation (wrong owner) was incomplete.
- Castafiore was **discarding that field**, so the only way to learn a playlist was read-only
  was to attempt a write and swallow the error — which is exactly what the old single-select
  did with `.catch(() => { })`. That is why this had been invisible until the new code logged
  it. Read-only rows are now dimmed with a lock and never written.
- Workaround for the user: edit the file on disk, or switch off the playlist's "Auto-Import"
  toggle in Navidrome to make it writable.

### Full-screen player: control cluster too tall
- `playerContainer` used `justifyContent: 'space-between'` across five children, so leftover
  height became four equal gaps. Tightening the controls therefore pushed the cluster *further
  from the cover* — the bottom region shrank by only 0.75x the amount removed. Moved the cover
  into a `coverSlot` (`flex: 1`) so the split is directly controllable; cover size untouched.
- Three measured passes on device (row analysis of 1080x2520 screenshots): centred/tight
  (cluster 452px, 17.9%) -> flex-end (fixed the gap but shoved the cover 233px down, worse) ->
  centred/roomier (cluster 562px, 22.3%, cover back up). Shipped the third.
- Queue view regressed to the bottom in the first pass (the slot centred its content); gave it
  a top-aligned `coverSlotTop` variant.
- Structural finding: the cover is width-capped at `width - 50` (361dp on 411x960dp), so it
  only fills ~37% of the height and the leftover void is unavoidable at a fixed cover size.
  Also noted: `compact = width < 420 || height < 700` is true on this tall phone purely on
  width, so it gets compact spacing and the smaller play button. Left alone — changing the
  threshold also resizes the play button.

### Also fixed
- `Settings/Downloads`: the active-download rows were direct children of the LegendList, so
  they missed both the 20px inset and the `maxWidth: 800` cap every other section has — they
  rendered full-bleed and stretched the window on tablet/desktop. Wrapped in the same
  `contentMainContainer`.

Verification: eslint clean; assembleRelease OK; installed on R5CY71GYKWF; no crash; layout and
queue view confirmed by screenshot. Tablet (R52Y80837NY) has been off USB since 2026-09-26
and is still on fbc0beb.

## 2026-09-26 — Radio as a continuous sampler + download/cache identity across 2 servers
Two follow-ups to the playback-correctness pass. TODO #32, #33.

### Radio: "plays the same 50 in loop, I want continuous sampling"
- Root cause: the top-up only ran once the last track ENDED, so the fetch landed in the gap
  between batches — an audible dead pause is what made it sound chunked. And a failed top-up fell
  through to `(index+1) % length`. Compounded by `repeatQueue` being stored as `true` on existing
  installs, so the wrap was ALWAYS taken. That last part is the direct answer to "same 50 in loop":
  getSettings does `{...defaultSettings, ...data}`, so my earlier default change never reached the
  device. Fixed with a versioned migration (v2) that forces repeatQueue off once.
- `playerCore`: RADIO_LOW_WATER=10 low-water top-up (never in the gap), batch 20 / fallback page
  100, shared `radioPending` promise so the kick-off and the end-of-queue net await one fetch.
- A radio session NEVER wraps: endOfTrackAction returns 'next' for radioMode, and an exhausted pool
  reshuffles the whole allowed set (repeats are fine for a sampler; replaying one batch is not).
- RADIO_MAX_QUEUE=200 then restart the session on a fresh pool via `setQueue` + `keepUpNext`.
  Chosen over trimming played tracks off the head, which would mean re-deriving song.index, the
  queue rotation and every drag-and-drop index — the user asked for the easier option.
- `#<user>-exclusive` filtered in BOTH sampling paths (unseen pool and reshuffle fallback).
- Visibility: Radio badge next to shuffle (tap ends it) + "End radio" in player options.
  radioMode was invisible, which is exactly why an endless session read as a bug.
- RandomButton (Album/Playlist/Favorited), Artist, Genre no longer set radioMode — they play their
  collection and stop. Shuffling a 12-track album used to silently become endless library randoms.
- Verified: 7-assertion node harness (migration forces stale stored true off; exhausted pool
  reshuffles instead of returning empty; exclusions absolute in the repeat path; in-page
  duplicate ids deduped). eslint clean, release APK builds + installs, no crash.

### Download/cache mismatch between the two server addresses
User confirmed the two servers are the SAME library behind two addresses, and NetworkAutoSwitch
flips between them automatically. Found three distinct mechanisms:
1. `maxBitRate` was never part of the cache identity (filename `${id}.${format}`, URL carried the
   bitrate) → a 128k transcode answered every 320k request forever. New `songCacheKey` adds the
   suffix, but ONLY for `format !== 'raw'`: urlStream returns a plain `stream?id=X` for raw, so
   keying on bitrate there would re-download the library byte-for-byte on every slider move. Raw is
   the default, so the common case is byte-identical to before — no migration, no re-download.
2. No provenance at all. buildMeta now records `server`; never used to invalidate (same library,
   re-fetching on a switch would burn the data plan) but it makes a mismatch visible, and it lets
   meta.cover be re-pointed at the active server — it was a baked absolute URL, so after a switch
   it pointed at the LAN host and 404'd off the house wifi.
3. songUrl baked global.config at enqueue, so queued items kept the old address after a switch.
   initDownloads (already re-run on every config change) re-points queued/paused items; in-flight
   ones keep their URL because the bytes are still valid and cancelling wastes a partial download.
- Also: partUri derived in runDownload not enqueue (no stranded .part on a mid-queue bitrate
  change); initCacheSong prunes non-songCacheKey files; dead `global.config.folderCache` removed.
- Verified: 15-assertion node harness over the verbatim rules — the important one being that
  raw+bitrate-change yields an UNCHANGED key while mp3 128 vs 320 differ, and a 128 file does not
  answer a 320 query (isSongCached is an exact array lookup).
- **Left alone, flagged to the user:** NetworkAutoSwitch still flips mid-song and the current track
  is deliberately NOT interrupted (cached or already streaming). Offered to change it.

## 2026-09-26 — FIX: playback correctness pass (background / looping / wrong cover)
- Report: "problems with the app running in the background, songs looping, not correct update of
  the cover, and sometimes playlist looping". Treated the three symptoms as hints and audited the
  whole playback path: queue reducer, `playerCore`, all 4 backends, the headless service, and the
  player components. Two parallel subagent sweeps (playback-logic divergence + UI state
  inconsistency) produced the inventory; root causes below.
- Baseline commit `ba8b982` (checkpointed the previously uncommitted reconcile/Tier-3 work first,
  per AGENTS.md). Plan: `plans/playback-correctness.md`. TODO #31.
- **Queue-row double play (mobile)** — `SongItem` decided whether to *also* `playSong` from
  `onPress`'s return value; `FullScreenPlayer`'s queue rows returned a Promise (always truthy) →
  one tap ran `setIndex` AND `playSong` → two `load()`s of one track (restart from 0) and a
  `setQueue` that wiped `upNext`/`originalQueue`/`radioMode` and reset `actionEndOfSong` to 'next'.
  Desktop was never affected — `FullScreenHorizontalPlayer` uses its own `QueueRow` (a plain
  `Pressable`), not `SongItem`, so the earlier claim that both players were affected was wrong.
  Replaced the truthiness contract with an explicit `playsOnPress` prop so it can't regress.
- **Radio top-up PREPENDED** — `extendRadio` dispatched `addToQueue` with no `index` and the
  reducer only guarded `index === null`, so `splice(undefined)` coerced to 0: every 50-track
  top-up rebuilt the queue at the FRONT in reverse order and left `index` pointing at a different
  track. This is very likely the actual "playlist loops" + wrong now-playing cause. Fixed the
  guard to `typeof action.index !== 'number'` and deduped within the fetched page (getRandomSongs
  repeats ids, and those played back-to-back).
- **Wrong cover after resume** — up-next playback moves `songInfo` without moving `index`, so
  `reconcileState`'s `findIndex(active.id)` returned -1 and it bailed WITHOUT dispatching → the UI
  kept the previous song's title/cover. Now heals via `syncGlobal` with the native track.
  `useQueueDnD` also anchors the rotation on songInfo's real position instead of the stale index.
- **Up next wiped on every navigation** — `setIndex` cleared `upNext` unconditionally, so a plain
  next/previous, a drag, or a resume reconcile deleted the list. Now opt-in via
  `clearUpNext`/`clearRadio`, set only by an explicit queue-row jump.
- **Inconsistency sweep result**: the end-of-track decision existed 5× and had drifted —
  cast/upnp seeked to 0 without resuming (repeat-one silently stopped), web had NO stop branch,
  the end-of-queue predicate was duplicated with/without the radio+upNext guards, scrobble
  ordering differed. Consolidated into `endOfTrackAction()` + `scrobbleTrack()` +
  `stopAtQueueEnd()` in `playerCore`; mechanics stay per backend. Also: `convertState` existed
  3× — `playerLocal` had no `State.Loading`/`Connecting` branch (reported Stopped, no spinner)
  and `playerCast` used `playerState`'s `State.Stopped` ('stopped') as a GoogleCast lookup key,
  which is not a key → `undefined` → dropped by the reducer (no error icon on Chromecast).
  `setPosition` clamping differs 4×; `downloadNextSong` arity differs (2 native vs 3 web);
  `restoreState` plays before seeking on 3 of 4 backends; `playerUpnp.disconnect(device)` shadowed
  the module-level `device`.
- **repeatQueue default → false** (user). Playback stops at the end of a queue unless repeat is
  on; a failed radio top-up stops instead of silently wrapping. This exposed a dead play button
  (`isSongLoad` stayed true → `resumeSong()` on an ENDED track) → new `song.queueEnded` flag
  makes play restart the queue. Also fixed a missing `else` in `PlayButton.onPress` that ran
  `resumeSong()` right after `playSong()`.
- **App swipe-away stops playback — kept as-is** (user decision), but now an explicit commented
  choice rather than an accident. Background playback while merely backgrounded is unaffected.
  Dropped `alwaysPauseOnInterruption: true`: it made the service's `RemoteDuck` handler dead code
  (the OS had already paused, so `state === State.Playing` never held) and left playback paused
  forever after any notification. `progressUpdateEventInterval: -1` → `1` (-1 had disabled the
  Android notification's progress bar/scrubber; `useProgress` polls on its own).
- **`initPlayer` could leave the app playerless for a whole session**: it returned early on
  `android_cannot_setup_player_in_background` BEFORE dispatching `init`, and `SongProvider`
  already `remove()`d its AppState listener → `song.isInit` false forever → no resume reconcile,
  no retry, no player options. Now returns before `init` only for a real failure and the provider
  retries on every foreground (it also inits immediately when the app is already active, which
  the old change-event-only path could miss entirely).
- **Reducer consistency** — `addToQueue`/`addToUpNext` never ran `convertTrack`, so the queue
  mixed raw-API and converted shapes and every reducer action re-serialized megabytes to
  AsyncStorage. `convertTrack` now keeps `isLiveStream` (the downloader was trying to cache live
  radio streams forever; the seek bar showed a finite duration for them) + `albumArtists`/
  `discNumber`. `setRating` now also writes `starred` so queue-row hearts update.
- **Hooks** — `updateGlobalSettings()` called 7 `useEffect`s from a plain async function during
  render; worked only because the call order happened to be stable. Moved into
  `SettingsProvider` as real effects. (`eslint-plugin-react-hooks` is still not installed.)
- **Lists/Lyric** — index `keyExtractor`s replaced with occurrence-aware keys in both queue views,
  `Lyric`, `Favorited`, `Playlist` (index keys made rows carry the previous occupant's download
  icon after a rotation/search/sort; plain ids would collide on a duplicated track). `Lyric`
  re-fetched on every unrelated dispatch and could paint the previous track's lyrics.
- **Misc** — unguarded `global.song.queue` in the service's `PlaybackActiveTrackChanged` threw on a
  cold headless start and took the scrobble with it; `TimeBar` deps omitted the track;
  `ImageError` stored a nullish `source` and threw on the next run; `saveQueue` built a multi-KB URL
  for big queues; `RemoteProvider` ran `playSong(config, dispatch, null, 0)` (TypeError) when
  connecting with no queue and read a stale React snapshot instead of `global.song`.
- `npm run eslint` clean; `assembleRelease` OK (Metro bundle resolves all imports).
- **On-device verify still owed**: queue-row tap no longer restarts the track; radio top-up
  appends; cover/title correct after a screen-off resume during up-next playback; Up next survives
  next/prev; a queue stops at the end and play restarts it; repeat-one works on cast/upnp.
- Deferred on purpose: `Player.updateTime()` still runs all three backends' hooks (2
  `useProgress(500)` pollers + an always-on UPnP progress listener) — switching the hook set by
  device type would change the hook count per component and break React's rules. `useCachedFirst`
  still has no in-flight `uid` guard (unlike `useCachedAndApi`).

## 2026-08-28 — Fix: next/prev loops on same song (icons + Bluetooth, not swipe)
- Symptom: pressing next/prev (full-screen transport / notification / Bluetooth) sometimes plays
  the same song from the start 3-5× before advancing; swipe never does.
- Root cause: commit `d8ffc98` (KISS refactor) changed native `setIndex` from fire-and-forget
  load + immediate dispatch to `await loadSong` + deferred dispatch. During the load window
  `global.song.index` stays stale → a re-trigger (natural `PlaybackQueueEnded` near song end,
  Bluetooth double event, fast double-tap) issues a second `load()` of the same track → the
  song restarts until the deferred dispatch lands. Verified the KotlinAudio fork only emits
  `AudioPlayerState.ENDED` (→ `PlaybackQueueEnded`) on genuine end of playback, and `MusicService`
  emits QueueEnded on every `ENDED` transition → the cascade.
- "Not swipe": swipes land mid-song (no ENDED, single invocation); icons/Bluetooth are pressed
  at/near song end and double-fire.
- Fix: `playerCore.setIndex` dedupes in-flight loads of the same track id (`loadingKey`,
  try/finally cleared) — a repeat request for an already-loading track only dispatches state.
  One shared chokepoint covers local/cast/upnp/web auto-advance routes
  (`servicePlayback.PlaybackQueueEnded`, cast `onMediaPlaybackEnded`, upnp `TRACK_ENDED`, web `ended`).
- eslint clean. Plan: `plans/fix-next-prev-looping.md`. TODO #29. On-device verify pending.
- Not fixed (known, benign): (a) different-target rapid double-skip still briefly races two
  distinct loads (pre-existing `load()` semantics, not a loop); (b) repeat-ON transient restart
  during the window before `setRepeat('next')` lands. UpNext branch unaffected (loads directly).

