## 2026-08-29 — FIX: UI lags actual playback after screen-off resume
- Report: "when the screen turns off, song alternates; when I turn it on again it's
  misaligned with the actual state and I have to catch the actual one."
- Root cause: AUTO-ADVANCE ONLY RUNS IN THE HEADLESS SERVICE (servicePlayback.js) and
  dispatches via fakeSongDispatch = (action) => songReducer(global.song, action) — mutates
  global.song, NEVER React state. Lock-screen notification skips (RemoteNext/Previous) do
  the same. React's only catch-up was useEvent's PlaybackActiveTrackChanged drift-compare
  (playerLocal.native.js), which is unreliable while backgrounded (throttled/dozed JS), so
  the UI stayed pinned on the old index/song until a manual next/prev forced a setIndex.
- Fix: new LocalPlayer.reconcileState(songDispatch) — reads TrackPlayer.getActiveTrack()
  (native truth); if active.id matches global.song.songInfo.id -> dispatch 'syncGlobal'
  (heals queue/index/songInfo/upNext); else map active.id into global.song.queue -> setIndex.
  Exported via player.native (optional-call `?.` = no-op for cast/upnp/web). SongProvider
  gained an AppState listener: on 'active', when song.isInit, Player.reconcileState?.(dispatch).
- Decision: notification skip buttons keep fakeSongDispatch for now (per user); reconcile
  heals React on resume. Upgrade path if lock-screen liveness ever matters: use
  global.songDispatch (real React dispatch, set in initPlayer) in the service when app alive.
- Verify pending: eslint clean; build+install (connected device only). Manual test: play a
  queue, screen off, let a few tracks advance, unlock -> title/artwork/queue highlight must
  already match audio. Plan: plans/background-reconcile.md.

