### 2026-10-18 (cont.) — the first release launch crashed (TODO #40)
- Installed that APK on phone R5CY71GYKWF: it died instantly and repeatedly
  (`Invariant Violation: "main" has not been registered`) — a release-only regression.
- Root cause: in `PlayerBackground.native.js` the shader "comment" line inside the
  FRAGMENT_SHADER **template literal** contained backticks:
  `// Normalization matters: plain \`* 0.5 + 0.5\` leaves the tails ...`
  A backtick closes the template early, so everything after it was parsed as JS:
  `... * 0.5 + 0.5` followed by another backtick starts a **tagged template**, i.e. a
  call with the number `0.5` as the tag function. That is the whole story:
  `TypeError: 0.5 is not a function` while the module body ran, at load, before
  `AppRegistry.registerComponent`. Fix: drop the backticks from that comment.
- Diagnostic recipe (keep!): the release stack is Hermes **bytecode offsets**, not JS
  positions. Two commands turn it into a real file:line —
  1. `npx expo export:embed --platform android --dev false --reset-cache --minify false
     --entry-file index.js --bundle-output /tmp/device.js --sourcemap-output /tmp/device.map`
     reproduces the exact JS the build feeds to hermesc (the gradle task passes
     `--reset-cache` and `--minify false`: with Hermes, Metro does not minify, Hermes -O
     optimizes — see `@react-native/gradle-plugin` TaskConfiguration.kt:76);
  2. `hermesc -w -emit-binary -max-diagnostic-width=80 -O -output-source-map -out device.hbc
     device.js` reproduces the artifact (2068844 bytes, same as the APK), and its
     `device.hbc.map` maps the trace offset back to a bundle position — then a line of
     `device.js` shows the offending expression.
- Both earlier theories were wrong and are retired: (a) Metro/terser inlining `GAIN` into
  `.5.toFixed(1)` only appears in `--minify true` bundles we do not ship (verified with
  hermesc that leading-dot literals do break Hermes, hence `glslFloat()` stays as cheap
  insurance, but it was never the crash), and (b) the GLView/expo-gl import was innocent.
- Lesson: never put backticks (or `${` text) in a JS comment *inside* a template literal;
  the shader strings are the risky spot. Verify shader source by extracting the
  transformed template and running it in node (asserts the GLSL floats came out right).

### 2026-10-18 (cont.) — second crash of the same build: R8 vs expo-gl's GLView
- With the JS fixed the app boots, but it crash-loops on a **native** exception the first
  time the GLView is actually rendered:
  `IllegalStateException: Didn't find a correct constructor for class I6.d` inside
  `expo.modules.kotlin.views...SimpleViewManagerWrapper.createViewInstance`.
- `I6.d` is the obfuscated name of `expo.modules.gl.GLView` (looked up in
  `android/app/build/outputs/mapping/release/mapping.txt`, kept after release builds).
- Why: expo creates its views **reflectively** and looks for a `(Context)` or
  `(Context, AppContext)` constructor. `expo-gl`'s GLView extends `TextureView`
  *directly* (not expo's `ExpoView` base class), and expo-gl ships **no** consumer
  ProGuard rules, while `expo-modules-core`'s generic rule only covers
  `implements ExpoView` classes. With `minifyEnabled=true` (gradle.properties) R8 sees
  the record constructor as unused and strips it → reflective lookup fails. The app then
  crashes at first player render, restarts, and loops.
- Fix: one keep rule in `android/app/proguard-rules.pro` (rule text explains itself).
  Class is referenced by the autolinked package list, so keeping the single constructor
  is enough; no rule needed for `GLObjectManagerModule` (covered by the generic Module
  rules already).
- Lesson for any future expo module with a native view: check whether the view class
  conforms to expo's `ExpoView` base or to `kotlin.reflect` construction, and add the
  matching keep rule the moment a release build renders it. Diagnose fast via
  mapping.txt (`grep "obfuscated_name ->" mapping.txt`).

### 2026-10-18 (cont.) — third crash of the same build: R8 vs GLContext.flush (name-based JNI)
- With the view class kept, the next launch reached the first GL frame and then the process
  *aborted natively*: `JNI DETECTED ERROR IN APPLICATION: mid == null` inside
  `expo.modules.gl.cpp.EXGL.EXGLContextPrepare`.
- Cause: expo-gl's C++ bridge resolves `GLContext.flush` **by name** at runtime
  (`node_modules/expo-gl/android/src/main/cpp/EXGLJniApi.cpp:29`:
  `GetMethodID(GLContextClass, "flush", "()V")`). R8 had renamed the method, the lookup
  returned a null jmethodID, and the JNI call aborted the process.
- Fix: `-keepclassmembers class expo.modules.gl.GLContext { void flush(); }`; confirmed in
  `mapping.txt` (`void flush():116:116 -> flush`).
- Lesson: a constructor keep rule is not enough for expo modules whose C++ looks members up
  by string — those need `-keepclassmembers` too.
- Durability: `android/` is fully gitignored (prebuild output) — both rules would vanish on
  the next `expo prebuild`. Added `plugins/proguardRules.js` (a `withDangerousMod` plugin
  wired in `app.config.js`) that re-appends the custom keep block when missing.
- On-device verification of the feature: zero FATALs, `[PlayerBackground] context
  1080x2520`, palette extracted from the local cover, animation confirmed by frame-diff,
  player + queue screenshots correct. User verdict: "tutto perfetto".

### 2026-10-18 (cont.) — "gold artifact": frozen top strip in the player background
- The user kept pointing at a gold/gold-ish band at the top of the player. Frame-diff proof:
  that strip is pixel-identical across screenshots 1.4 s apart (0.0 delta) while bands below
  animate — i.e. it is *never repainted*, and shows pixels from an older palette (gold).
- Root cause: expo-gl creates the EGL surface **once**, from the size at mount time, and
  ignores all later surface-size changes (`GLView.kt` re-initializes only when the initial
  size was 0). Mounts that happen while the window is in another mode / mid-layout capture a
  bogus size (seen: `1313x2028` on a 1080x2520 display); every draw then lands in that
  buffer and the uncovered strip keeps whatever was drawn before — a *stale* region, not a
  shader artifact.
- Fix in `PlayerBackground.native.js`: self-measure (`onLayout`: dp → px via PixelRatio)
  and remount the `GLView` while `gl.drawingBufferWidth/Height` don't match the measured
  size (capped at 5 attempts, ≥500 ms apart, each logged); `context WxH (view VxV)` at
  every context creation. Suspected same root for "the background stops working" reports
  (a context created mid-layout draws only part of the view).
- Status: verified on device 2026-10-18 — 3/3 mounts `context 1080x2520 (view 1080x2520)`,
  top strip animates in frame-diff (was 0.0), player screenshot clean. Cause of the good
  result: the GLView now mounts only *after* the wrapper's first `onLayout` (the surface is
  created at the settled size); the capped remount stays as the fallback for mid-layout or
  cross-window cases.

### 2026-10-19 — Mini player ("pill"): optional fBM background
- User request: same fBM field behind the mini player. Decisions (asked, not assumed):
  setting in **Settings → Theme**, **off by default**, **user-chosen FPS** (10/15/20/30,
  default 15), animation keeps running while the music is paused.
- Key enabler found while planning: expo-gl's Android `GLView` extends **`TextureView`**
  (`node_modules/expo-gl/android/src/main/java/expo/modules/gl/GLView.kt:18`), not a
  `SurfaceView`. So the pill's `borderRadius` + `overflow: hidden` clips the GL output and
  the card's translate gestures move it like a normal view — no z-order/rounding hacks.
- Implementation: `PlayerBackground` gained `fps` (frame gate `1000/fps`), `paused`
  (stops the rAF loop while the component stays mounted behind `display:none`) and
  `noiseScale` (`u_scale` uniform; default 1 leaves the full-screen shader unchanged, the
  short pill uses 1.6). `BoxPlayer` renders it behind a 0.18 theme scrim (dim 0.25) only
  when `miniPlayerBackground` is on and Reduce transparency is off; `paused` = dismissed
  or keyboard open. Theme screen: switch + FPS list; new defaults
  `miniPlayerBackground:false`, `miniPlayerFps:15` (merged over defaults at load, no
  settingsVersion bump needed).
- Contrast: palette is already luminance-clamped for white text (feature 3); dim + theme
  scrim on top. Verify ≥ 4.5:1 on bright covers during the device pass.
- State: eslint + release build green. On-device verification pending — USB devices were
  unplugged mid-session (both phone and tablet disappeared from `lsusb`).

### 2026-10-18/19 — Idle CPU burn investigation (still open, see TODO #43)
- Symptom: ~20-35% CPU at idle on Home; main thread ~19%, `mqt_js` ~10%;
  `Choreographer#doFrame` ~120/s with ~0 HWUI `DrawFrame` → a per-vsync loop that paints
  nothing. Same on Settings/Music tabs, online and offline, after dismissing the pill.
- Ruled out by reading + measuring: Home-only cause, the mini player, `useKeyboardIsOpen`,
  `updateApi` churn (plain state), UPnP polling (starts only on cast connect), `useProgress`
  / Cast `useStreamPosition` (player screens are not mounted; `Player.js` swaps the pill for
  the full-screen player), LegendList (its rAF paths are scroll/event-driven and no
  LegendList is mounted on the tested screens), `Animated` loops (no `Animated.loop` in app
  code; the 8 Animated users are event-driven/native-driver).
- `simpleperf` is blocked (app not profileable, no root). Perfetto is available but needs a
  different angle. Next step: instrumented build — patch saved at
  `/tmp/opencode/temp-debug-raf-hunt.patch` (wraps global `requestAnimationFrame`,
  `setTimeout`/`setInterval` and the `Animated` factories, logs counts + a stack every
  3 s and needs `metro.config.js` `minifierConfig {mangle:false}` for readable names).
  Diagnostic APK was built and ready when the devices were unplugged; rebuild from the
  patch to resume.
- On-device result: user ran the feature themselves (install `bb9fc10` via USB, then the cable
  dropped again). Pill animation, open/close player, track change: all OK. Logs had already
  confirmed the pill GL surface (`context 1024x158`) and palette re-extraction per player mount.
  Status: verified.
